  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-posterior-predictive-law": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$ and $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$ be measurable spaces. Suppose $\\Pi$ is a supplied probability kernel from $\\mathsf Y$ to $\\Theta$; write its probability measure at $y$ as $\\Pi_y$. Let $Q$ be a probability kernel from $\\Theta$ to a measurable future-observation space $(\\mathsf Z,\\mathcal Z)$. Define the **posterior-predictive probability kernel** by\n$$Q_y^{\\mathrm{pred}}(B):=\\int_\\Theta Q_\\theta(B)\\,\\Pi_y(d\\theta),\\qquad y\\in\\mathsf Y,\\ B\\in\\mathcal Z.$$\nA probability kernel has pointwise probability-measure sections and measurable event evaluations, as in [[def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel]]. This definition takes the posterior kernel as supplied; it does not form a likelihood quotient.\n\nFor a proper-prior dominated model, [[thm-bayes-formula-for-dominated-kernels]] supplies one such data-indexed kernel when the sampling laws have a specified nonnegative jointly measurable density against a sigma-finite data measure. Its stated filler on zero or infinite normalizers is part of that kernel version."
    ],
    "thm-posterior-predictive-mixture-is-a-kernel": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\Pi$ be a probability kernel from $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$ to $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$ and let $Q$ be a probability kernel from $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$ to $(\\mathsf Z,\\mathcal Z)$. For\n$$\nQ_y^{\\mathrm{pred}}(B)=\\int_\\Theta Q_\\theta(B)\\,\\Pi_y(d\\theta),\n$$\neach $Q_y^{\\mathrm{pred}}$ is a probability measure, the evaluations $y\\mapsto Q_y^{\\mathrm{pred}}(B)$ are measurable, and, for every nonnegative measurable $g:\\mathsf Z\\to[0,\\infty]$,\n$$\n\\int_{\\mathsf Z} g(z)\\,Q_y^{\\mathrm{pred}}(dz)=\\int_\\Theta\\left(\\int_{\\mathsf Z}g(z)\\,Q_\\theta(dz)\\right)\\Pi_y(d\\theta).\n$$"
    ],
    "thm-posterior-predictive-moment-decomposition": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\Pi_y$ be a supplied posterior probability measure on $\\Theta$ and $Q_\\theta$ a future probability kernel on $\\mathbb R$. Put\n$$s_2(\\theta)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}z^2\\,Q_\\theta(dz),$$and assume $\\int_\\Theta s_2(\\theta)\\,\\Pi_y(d\\theta)<\\infty$. On the full-measure set $E_y=\\{\\theta:s_2(\\theta)<\\infty\\}$, define$$\\mu(\\theta)=\\int z\\,Q_\\theta(dz),\\qquad v(\\theta)=\\operatorname{Var}_{Q_\\theta}(Z),$$and set $\\mu=v=0$ off $E_y$ when integrating them against $\\Pi_y$. Then$$\\int z\\,Q_y^{\\mathrm{pred}}(dz)=\\int\\mu(\\theta)\\,\\Pi_y(d\\theta),$$\n$$\\operatorname{Var}_{Q_y^{\\mathrm{pred}}}(Z)=\\int v(\\theta)\\,\\Pi_y(d\\theta)+\\operatorname{Var}_{\\Pi_y}(\\mu).$$\nThese kernel identities are choice-free. For the conditional interpretation, suppose a joint probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$ carries measurable random elements $Y$ (data), $X$ (parameter), and $Z$ (future observation). Let $\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal H\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ be sub-sigma-algebras such that $Y$ is $\\mathcal G$-measurable and $X$ is $\\mathcal H$-measurable. Assume $\\Pi_{Y}$ is a regular conditional distribution of $X$ given $\\mathcal G$ and $Q_X$ is a regular conditional distribution of $Z$ given $\\mathcal H$. Thus the future law given the information in $\\mathcal H$ is assumed to depend on it through $X$ alone. If $\\mathbb E[Z^2]<\\infty$, then $Q^{\\mathrm{pred}}_Y$ is a regular conditional distribution of $Z$ given $\\mathcal G$, and the displayed identities give the corresponding conditional-mean and conditional-variance versions almost surely. Here conditional variance means a version of the conditional expectation of the squared deviation from the corresponding conditional-mean version, using [[def-conditional-expectation-given-a-sigma-algebra]]. Identifying those versions with conditional-expectation equivalence classes uses AC, as stated below. Values on null data fibers remain version-dependent."
    ],
    "def-posterior-credible-set": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$ and $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$ be measurable spaces and let $y\\mapsto\\Pi_y$ be a supplied posterior probability kernel. For $\\alpha\\in[0,1]$, a set-valued rule $C$ is **graph-measurable** when\n$$\\{(y,\\theta)\\in\\mathsf Y\\times\\Theta:\\theta\\in C(y)\\}\\in\\mathcal Y\\otimes\\mathcal T.$$\nIt is a **posterior credible set at level $1-\\alpha$** when it is graph-measurable and\n$$\\Pi_y(C(y))\\ge 1-\\alpha\\qquad\\text{for every }y\\text{ in its declared data domain}.$$\nThe posterior-mass function is measurable by integration of the indicator of the membership graph against the kernel. If a density is available relative to a specified dominating measure, a highest-posterior-density region selects parameter values with largest density; such regions need not be intervals and may be disconnected or nonunique."
    ],
    "lem-measurable-posterior-quantile-and-cdf-bracketing": [
      "## Statement\n\nWrite $\\mathbb Q$ for the canonical image of the rationals in $\\mathbb R$ under the embedding of [[lem-rat-embeds-dense]].\n\nLet $y\\mapsto\\Pi_y$ be a probability kernel on $\\mathbb R$. For $u\\in(0,1)$ put\n$$F_y(t)=\\Pi_y((-\\infty,t]),\\qquad q_u(y)=\\inf\\{r\\in\\mathbb Q:F_y(r)\\ge u\\}.$$The infimum is taken in $\\mathbb R$; [[thm-infimum-property]] supplies it once the defining set is shown nonempty and bounded below. Then $q_u:\\mathsf Y\\to\\mathbb R$ is measurable, and$$\\Pi_y((-\\infty,q_u(y)))\\le u\\le\\Pi_y((-\\infty,q_u(y)]).$$"
    ],
    "thm-central-posterior-quantile-interval-is-credible": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\Pi_y$ be a posterior probability kernel on $\\mathbb R$ and let $0<\\alpha<1$. Define $C_\\alpha(y)=[q_{\\alpha/2}(y),q_{1-\\alpha/2}(y)]$ using the quantiles from [[lem-measurable-posterior-quantile-and-cdf-bracketing]]. Then $C_\\alpha$ is graph-measurable and $\\Pi_y(C_\\alpha(y))\\ge1-\\alpha$ for every $y$. Thus equal-tailed posterior quantile intervals are one kind of credible set."
    ],
    "def-posterior-median": [
      "## Definition\n\nFor a probability measure $\\Pi_y$ on $\\mathbb R$, a **posterior median** is any $m\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfying\n$$\\Pi_y((-\\infty,m])\\ge \\tfrac12\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\Pi_y([m,\\infty))\\ge\\tfrac12.$$\nThe two inequalities, rather than a density-based definition, apply to discrete, continuous, and mixed posteriors and permit more than one median."
    ],
    "lem-lower-posterior-median-is-measurable": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\Pi$ be a posterior probability kernel on $\\mathbb R$. The rule $m(y)=q_{1/2}(y)$ is measurable and is a posterior median on every data fiber."
    ],
    "def-posterior-risk-and-bayes-action": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$ have a supplied posterior probability kernel $\\Pi_y$, let $(\\mathsf A,\\mathcal G)$ be a measurable action space, and let $L:\\Theta\\times\\mathsf A\\to[0,\\infty]$ be a measurable function on the product measurable space [[def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras]], in the sense of [[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]]. This strengthens the section-measurability convention in [[def-loss-function-and-risk]] so that posterior integrals vary measurably with measurable action rules.\n\nFor a fixed data fiber $y$ and action $a$, define the **posterior risk** only when\n$$\\rho_y(a):=\\int_\\Theta L(\\theta,a)\\,\\Pi_y(d\\theta)<\\infty.$$\nA **pointwise Bayes action** at $y$ is a finite-risk action attaining the minimum among all finite-risk actions. On a declared measurable data domain $D$, a **Bayes action rule** is a measurable map $\\delta:D\\to\\mathsf A$ whose finite posterior risk attains that minimum at every $y\\in D$. This definition makes no assertion that finite-risk actions or measurable minimizing rules always exist. It therefore does not infer a Bayes action merely from the existence of an infimum. The sampling risk in [[def-loss-function-and-risk]] is a different integral, taken over the data law at a fixed parameter."
    ],
    "thm-posterior-mean-is-squared-error-bayes-action": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC. Let $\\Pi$ be a probability kernel from a measurable data space $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$ to $\\mathbb R$, and put\n$$D_2=\\left\\{y:\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\theta^2\\,\\Pi_y(d\\theta)<\\infty\\right\\},\\qquad \\mu(y)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\theta\\,\\Pi_y(d\\theta)\\quad(y\\in D_2).$$\nThen $D_2\\in\\mathcal Y$, $\\mu$ is measurable on $D_2$, and for every $y\\in D_2$, $\\mu(y)$ is the unique action minimizing the finite squared posterior risk $\\int(\\theta-a)^2\\Pi_y(d\\theta)$ over $a\\in\\mathbb R$. Thus $\\mu$ is a measurable Bayes action rule on $D_2$."
    ],
    "thm-posterior-median-minimizes-absolute-risk": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\Pi$ be a posterior probability kernel on $\\mathbb R$ and let\n$$D_1=\\left\\{y:\\int_{\\mathbb R}|\\theta|\\,\\Pi_y(d\\theta)<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThen $D_1$ is measurable. For every $y\\in D_1$, every posterior median minimizes the finite absolute posterior risk $a\\mapsto\\int|\\theta-a|\\,\\Pi_y(d\\theta)$. The measurable lower-median rule from [[lem-lower-posterior-median-is-measurable]] is therefore an attained Bayes action rule on $D_1$."
    ],
    "thm-finite-class-maximal-mass-is-zero-one-bayes-action": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\Pi$ be a posterior probability kernel on a nonempty finite class $\\Theta=\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$ with the sigma-algebra of all its subsets; give the action class the same measurable structure and use zero–one loss $L(\\theta,a)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\theta\\ne a\\}}$. Every posterior-mass maximizer minimizes pointwise posterior risk. The least-indexed posterior-mass maximizer is a measurable, finite-risk Bayes action rule. This statement is choice-free."
    ],
